BBsBhDīp_1,2.2.9:
nanvapsaṃvatsasṛṣṭyādinātrocyamānenāpi kathamasya viṣṇuprakaraṇatvaniścaya ityato 'psaṃvatsarasṛṣṭyāderviṣṇuliṅgatvāditi bhāvena tasya talliṅgatve smṛtimāha - nehāsīditi // 'ādau'; pralaye 'iha'; asvatantravarge 'kiñcināpi'; jīvo jaḍaṃ vā nāsīt / nanvidamayuktaṃ, jīvādeḥ nityasya sattvādityata āha - mṛtyuriti / pralaye mṛtyunāmā hariḥ jīvādyāvarakatvenāsīt / ata āvṛtatvānnāsīdityuktamiti nānupapattiriti bhāvaḥ / 'saḥ ātmanaḥ'; iti padacchedaḥ / 'mantreṣvāṅyāderātmanaḥ'; ityāllopaḥ / tathā ca 'saḥ'; prabhurviṣṇurātmano 'manasā'; svecchayaivāpo 'srākṣīt / 'tāsu'; apsu 'mahattaraṃ'; svārthe tarap / mahat sthūlam 'aṇḍaṃ'; brahmāṇḍaṃ nirmame / 'tatra'; aṇḍe saṃvatsarākhyaṃ brahmāṇamasṛjadityanvayaḥ nāmetyanena samyagvatsabhūtān ramayatīti vyutpattyā saṃvatsaraśabdaścaturmukhe prasiddha ityucyate / atrāsāmarthyaparihārāya - prabhuriti / 'taṃ'; caturmukham 'attuṃ'; bhakṣayituṃ 'vyādadāt'; vyāvṛtat viṣṇuḥ / tadā brahmā 'virurāva'; bhāṇitiśabdamakarodityarthaḥ / heti asyārthasya śrutiprasiddhatāṃ sūcayati / atha ārāvakaraṇānantaraṃ 'tatkṛpayā'; tasmin caturmukhe kṛpayā viṣṇustamajagdhvā sṛṣṭikriyāyāmayojayat / 'sa vibhuḥ'; caturmukho haraye hareradyārthaṃ bhuvanamasṛjat / yadvā - bhuvanamasṛjat, sṛṣṭaṃ taddharaye 'rpitavāniti śeṣeṇa yojanā brahmavaivarta ityanantaraṃ yata uktamiti śeṣaḥ / asyāto 'psaṃvatsarasṛṣṭyāderviṣṇuliṅgatvasiddhirityadhyāhṛtasādhyenānvayaḥ /
// iti attṛtvādhikaraṇam // 2 //

children.translated_text:
BBsBhDīp_1,2.2.9:
Now, even when [the context] is said to be established as a <s>Viṣṇu</s>-context through the creation of waters, <s>Saṃvatsara</s>, and the like, how is the determination that this is a <s>Viṣṇu</s>-context [achieved]? — therefore, with the intent of establishing through <s>Smṛti</s> that the creation of waters, <s>Saṃvatsara</s>, and the like are marks of <s>Viṣṇu</s>, he cites the <s>Smṛti</s>: '<s>neha</s>' (not here). // 'In the beginning' (<s>ādau</s>) — at the time of dissolution; 'here' (<s>iha</s>) — among the dependent class; 'not a thing whatsoever' (<s>kiñcit api</s>) — neither <s>jīva</s> nor insentient matter — existed. Now, this is unjustified, since the <s>jīvas</s>, being eternal, do exist — to this he says: '<s>mṛtyuḥ</s>' (Death). At dissolution, <s>Hari</s> bearing the name <s>Mṛtyu</s> existed as the enveloper of the <s>jīvas</s> and the like. Hence, because of being enveloped, 'did not exist' is said — so there is no impossibility: this is the import. '<s>Sa ātmanaḥ</s>' — this is the word-division. By the rule 'in <s>mantras</s>, the <s>ā</s> of <s>āṅ</s> and others is elided before <s>ātman</s>' — elision [occurs]. And thus, 'He' — the mighty Lord <s>Viṣṇu</s>, by His own will — 'by mind' (<s>manasā</s>) — alone — 'created the waters' (<s>apo asrākṣīt</s>). 'In those' (<s>tāsu</s>) waters, He fashioned a 'great' (<s>mahattaram</s>) — the comparative suffix being in the reflexive sense — a large, massive 'egg' (<s>aṇḍam</s>) — the <s>Brahmāṇḍa</s> (cosmic egg). 'Therein' (<s>tatra</s>) — in that egg — the syntactic connection is: He created <s>Brahmā</s> named <s>Saṃvatsara</s>. By the word '<s>nāma</s>' — [it is said that] by the etymology 'one who properly makes those who have become calves to delight,' the word '<s>Saṃvatsara</s>' is well-known as [referring to] the four-faced [<s>Brahmā</s>]. Here, to remove the [objection of] incapacity, [the word] '<s>prabhu</s>' (the mighty Lord) is used. 'Him' (<s>tam</s>) — the four-faced one — 'to eat' (<s>attum</s>) — to devour — <s>Viṣṇu</s> 'opened wide' (<s>vyādadāt</s>) — opened His mouth. Then <s>Brahmā</s> 'cried out' (<s>virurāva</s>) — made the sound '<s>bhāṇ</s>': this is the meaning. The particle 'ha' indicates the well-known-ness of this meaning in the <s>Śruti</s>. Then — after the act of crying out — 'out of compassion for him' (<s>tatkṛpayā</s>) — out of compassion toward that four-faced <s>Brahmā</s> — <s>Viṣṇu</s>, not devouring him, 'appointed' him to the work of creation. 'He, the mighty one' (<s>sa vibhuḥ</s>) — the four-faced <s>Brahmā</s> — created the world as 'food for <s>Hari</s>' (<s>adyārtham haraye</s>). Alternatively: he created the world; the remainder is: 'and offered the created [world] to <s>Hari</s>' — this is the alternative syntactic connection. After 'in the <s>Brahmavaivarta</s>,' the remainder is: 'because of being stated.' The syntactic connection with the interpolated thesis is: from this too, the establishment of the creation of waters, <s>Saṃvatsara</s>, and the like as marks of <s>Viṣṇu</s> [is achieved].
// Thus ends the <s>Attṛtvādhikaraṇa</s> (topic of the eater). // 2 //

'He is the lord, He is without rival, He is <s>Hari</s>, He is supreme, He is the most excellent;
when one pours clarified butter or water into this eye, it goes to the channels alone;
He is <s>Vāmana</s>, He is <s>Bhāmana</s>, He is bliss (<s>Ānanda</s>), He is <s>Acyuta</s>' — thus in the <s>Caturvedaśikhā</s>.

