BBsBhDīp_1,3.3.15:
nanu laukikaparimāṇādirāhityaṃ kathaṃ viṣṇoryuktaṃ, loke sarvapadārthānāṃ prākṛtaparimāṇasyaiva darśanādityata āha - asthūleti // 'asau puruṣottamaḥ'; bhagavān 'asthūlo 'naṇurūpo bhavati'; prākṛtalaukikasthūlāṇutvarūpadharmarahitavapurbhavati / evaṃ 'aviśvo viśvaśca'; lokabahirbhūto lokāntargataśca bhavati / tathā viruddhadharmarūpaśca bhavati / loke parasparaṃ viruddhāḥ sthūlatvāṇutvādayo dharmāḥ svarūpabhūtā yasya saḥ tathoktaḥ / kathamityato ghaṭakamāha - aiśvaryāditi // acintyaiśvaryasāmarthyādityarthaḥ / aiśvaryādevetyanena nātropapādakāntaramanveṣaṇīyamutyuktaṃ bhavati / iti brahmāṇḍe ityasya uktatvāt yuktā ityadhyāhāreṇa te dharmāḥ viṣṇoryuktā eveti pūrvavākyenānvayaḥ /
// iti akṣarādhikaraṇam // 3 //

children.translated_text:
BBsBhDīp 1,3.3.15:
Now, how can the absence of ordinary measure and the like be appropriate for <s>Viṣṇu</s>, since in the world all objects are seen to have only material measure? To this he says — beginning with '<s>asthūla</s>.' 'That <s>Puruṣottama</s>' — the Lord — 'is <s>asthūla</s> and <s>anaṇu</s> in form' — His body is devoid of the material, ordinary attributes of grossness and subtlety. Likewise, He 'is <s>aviśva</s> and <s>viśva</s>' — both outside the world and within the world. And He is 'of the form of contradictory attributes' — one whose essential nature comprises attributes that are mutually contradictory in the world, such as grossness and subtlety, is so described. How? To this he states the reconciling factor — '<s>aiśvaryāt</s>' (because of sovereignty): because of the power of inconceivable sovereignty — this is the meaning. By '<s>aiśvaryāt eva</s>' (because of sovereignty alone), it is stated that no other means of establishing [this] need be sought here. 'In the <s>Brahmāṇḍa</s>' is implied [as the source]; with the supplement 'they are appropriate,' the syntactic connection with the preceding sentence is: those attributes are indeed appropriate for <s>Viṣṇu</s>.
// Thus ends the <s>Akṣarādhikaraṇa</s> // 3 //

children.text:
BBsBhDīp_1,3.9.16:
nanu śūdrasya vedārthāvadhāraṇāderniṣiddhatve kathaṃ vidurādīnāṃ vaidikārthāvadhāraṇamasti / evaṃ saṃskārarahitānāṃ vedānadhikāre kathaṃ kṣatriyaḥ saṃskārarahitaḥ karṇaḥ nikhilāḥ śrutīradhijagau / na ca vācyamasāvapavādaviṣaya iti / avihitopanayanatve sati niṣiddhādhyayanānāṃ uttamastrītiryagādīnāmevāpavādaviṣayatvena niṣiddhādhyayanānāṃ śūdrāṇāṃ atadviṣayatvādityata āha - vidurādīnāmiti / ādipadena dharmavyādhādayo gṛhyante / tuśabdo viśeṣārthaḥ / tamevāha - utpannajñātvāditi / janmāntara eva vedātparamparayotpannamaparokṣajñānaṃ yeṣāṃ te tathoktāsteṣāṃ bhāvastattvam / tasmāt 'kaścit'; asādhāraṇaḥ itaraśūdrādibhyo vyāvṛtto viśeṣaḥ teṣvastītyarthaḥ / yata iti śeṣaḥ / ato na 'nāgniḥ'; ityādiniṣedhaviṣayatvaṃ teṣāṃ, kintu apavādaviṣayatvameveti na tatpratibandyetaraśūdrāṇāṃ vedādhikāraḥ śaṅkya iti bhāvaḥ / sūtrārthastu - śūdrasya śravaṇe 'trapujatubhyām'; iti vedaśravaṇādhyayanārthāvadhāraṇapratiṣedhakaśruteḥ 'nāgnirna yajñaḥ'; iti spaṣṭaṃ śūdrasyaiva vedādhyayananiṣedhakasmṛteśca na tasya tadabhāvo vedādhyayananiṣedhābhāva iti /
// iti apaśūdrādhikaraṇam // 9 //

children.translated_text:
Bhāṣyadīpikā on 1.3.9, verse 16:
Objection: If the ascertainment of Vedic meaning and the like is prohibited for the Śūdra, how do Vidura and the like possess ascertainment of Vedic meaning? Similarly, if those who lack saṃskāra (Vedic initiation) have no qualification for the Vedas, how did Karṇa, a Kṣatriya who lacked saṃskāra, master all the Śrutis? Nor should it be said that he is a subject of exception (apavāda) — because when upanayana is not prescribed, only those whose Vedic study is prohibited and who are exceptional beings (uttama) — women, animals, and the like — are subjects of exception, while Śūdras whose Vedic study is prohibited are not subjects of such exception. To this he says: 'of Vidura and the like.' // By the word 'ādi,' Dharmavyādha (the righteous hunter) and the like are included. The word 'tu' is for specification. He states that very specification — 'because of having arisen knowledge' (utpannajñātvāt). Those in whom direct knowledge (aparokṣajñāna) has arisen from the Veda through succession in a previous birth alone — they are so described; the reality of their state [is this]. Therefore 'some' — an uncommon, distinctive characteristic distinguished from other Śūdras and the like — exists in them: that is the meaning. 'Because' (yataḥ) is the remainder. Therefore the prohibition stated in 'No fire, no sacrifice' and the like does not apply to them; rather, they are subjects of exception alone — hence the Vedic qualification of other Śūdras should not be suspected based on them: this is the purport. The meaning of the sūtra, however, is: because of the Śruti 'When a Śūdra hears, [his ears should be filled] with lead and lac' — which prohibits hearing, studying, and ascertaining Vedic meaning for the Śūdra — and because of the smṛti 'No fire, no sacrifice' — which clearly prohibits Vedic study for the Śūdra alone — therefore there is no absence of that [prohibition], i.e., no absence of the prohibition of Vedic study [for them].
// Thus ends the Apaśūdrādhikaraṇa // 9 //

children.text:
BBsBhDīp_1,3.10.6:
nanūdyatatvaliṅgasya viṣṇau sāvakāśatve 'pi vajraśruterniravakāśatvātsvabhāvataḥ prabalatvācca kathaṃ niravakāśenāpi sarvaceṣṭakatvaliṅgena vajro viṣṇureveti nirṇaya ityataḥ śruterniravakāśatvasyaiva asiddherityāśayena pūrvapakṣyabhimatavajraśruterapi viṣṇāvavakāśaṃ smṛtyā darśayati - cakramiti / 'eṣaḥ'; bhagavān 'caṅkramaṇāt'; saṃsāre jīvaparibhrāmakatvāt cakram / yathoktaṃ tattvapradīpe - caṅkrāmayatīti cakramiti / evaṃ 'varjanāt'; daityairdūrādeva varjyate, na sanmukhīkriyate iti, sattarkadīpāvalīrītyā duṣṭairvarjyatvāt, sarvadoṣavarjanādvā vajramityucyate / 'khaṇḍanāt'; duṣṭāṅgacchedanāt damanācca khaḍga eṣa ityarthaḥ / evaśabdena tadabhedo vyāvartyate / tataḥ kim, eṣa iti ca kaḥ ityata āha - hetīti / evaṃ hariḥ 'svayaṃ'; sākṣāt anyasambandhamantareṇa 'hetināmā'; hetīnāṃ cakrādisarvāyudhānāṃ yāni nāmāni mukhyavācyatayā tadvānityarthaḥ / hetīti sāmānyagrahaṇena bhāṣye vajrapadamanyasyāpyupalakṣakamityuktaṃ bhavati/ brahmavaivarta ityanantaram uktamiti śeṣaḥ / ato na śrutivirodho 'pīti bhāvaḥ / 'ucyate hetināmā'; ityanena vajrādiśabdānāṃ viṣṇau vidvadrūḍhirapyastīti sūcitam / tena varjanādityuktayogasya rūḍherdairbalyāt ayoga iti nirastam/ na caivaṃ kāṭhakabhāṣye - vajravadbhayadaṃ ceti vajraśabdasya viṣṇau gauṇatvoktivirodha iti vācyam / tatra tadvākyasya yathākathañcit brahmaparatvasiddhirityatraiva tātparyāt / brahmaparatvasya gauṇyāpi siddheḥ gauṇatve tātparyābhāvāt / atra tu samanvayasūtrapratijñātamukhyārthatvāya tātparyato yogarūḍhyorevoktatvāt / sūtrārthastu spaṣṭaḥ /
// iti kampanādhikaraṇam // 10 //

children.translated_text:
BBsBhDīp_1,3.10.6:
Now, even though the indicative mark of being praised has scope for application to <s>Viṣṇu</s>, since the <s>śruti</s> (scripture) regarding <s>vajra</s> (thunderbolt) has no other scope and is inherently stronger, how can the conclusion be that <s>vajra</s> is <s>Viṣṇu</s> Himself even through the indicative mark of being the cause of all activity, which also has no other scope? To this, with the intention that the very claim of the <s>śruti</s> having no other scope is itself unestablished, he shows through <s>smṛti</s> (tradition) that even the <s>vajra</s>-<s>śruti</s> accepted by the <s>pūrvapakṣin</s> (objector) has scope for application to <s>Viṣṇu</s> — with the word '<s>cakra</s>' (discus). 'This one' — the Lord — is called '<s>cakra</s>' (discus) because of '<s>caṅkramaṇa</s>' (wandering), i.e., because He causes the <s>jīvas</s> (individual souls) to revolve in <s>saṃsāra</s> (cycle of rebirth). As stated in the <s>Tattvapradīpa</s>: 'Because He causes to wander (<s>caṅkrāmayati</s>), He is <s>cakra</s>.' Similarly, because of 'avoidance (<s>varjana</s>)' — because <s>daityas</s> (demons) avoid Him from afar and do not face Him, or according to the method of the <s>Sattarkadīpāvalī</s>, because He is avoided by the wicked, or because He is free from all faults (<s>sarvadoṣavarjana</s>), He is called <s>vajra</s>. Because of 'breaking (<s>khaṇḍana</s>)' — because of cutting the limbs of the wicked and subduing them, He is a sword (<s>khaḍga</s>) — this is the meaning. By the word 'eva' (indeed), non-difference from Him is indicated. Then what? And who is 'this one'? To this he says — '<s>hetī</s>' (weapon). Thus <s>Hari</s> 'Himself' — directly, without relation to anything else — is 'named after weapons (<s>hetināmā</s>)'; the names of all weapons such as the discus and others are His names in their primary denotation — this is the meaning. By the general term '<s>hetī</s>' (weapon), it is indicated in the <s>Bhāṣya</s> (commentary) that the word <s>vajra</s> is also suggestive of other weapons. The remainder is that this is stated in the <s>Brahmavaivarta</s> (Purāṇa). Therefore there is no contradiction with <s>śruti</s> — this is the purport. By the statement 'He is called <s>hetināmā</s> (named after weapons),' it is indicated that the words <s>vajra</s> and so forth have conventional usage (<s>vidvadrūḍhi</s>) referring to <s>Viṣṇu</s> as well. Thereby the objection that the etymological derivation stated as 'because of avoidance' is not applicable due to the weakness of conventional usage is refuted. Nor should it be said that this contradicts the statement in the <s>Kāṭhaka</s> commentary that the word <s>vajra</s> is used figuratively (<s>gauṇa</s>) for <s>Viṣṇu</s> in 'fear-inspiring like a thunderbolt.' There, the purport is only to establish that the passage somehow refers to <s>Brahman</s>; since the reference to <s>Brahman</s> is established even through figurative usage, there is no intent to assert figurative usage per se. But here, since the purport is stated through both etymological derivation (<s>yoga</s>) and conventional usage (<s>rūḍhi</s>) in order to establish the primary meaning as promised by the <s>Samanvaya-sūtra</s>. The meaning of the <s>sūtra</s> is clear.
// Thus ends the <s>Kampana-adhikaraṇa</s> (section on trembling) // 10 //

