BBsBhDīp_1,3.11.3:
If <s>jyotis</s> (light) is <s>Viṣṇu</s>, then there would be a contradiction with the indicative mark of wandering through both worlds — to this doubt also, he resolves it through the <s>śruti</s> indicated by the word '<s>darśana</s>' (seeing) in the <s>sūtra</s> — with '<s>Prājña</s> (the all-knowing).' 'Because of the statement' — from the <s>Vājasaneya</s> scriptural passage — 'of Him also' — of the <s>Paramātmā</s> also — wandering through worlds exists indeed — this is the meaning. 'Not of the <s>jīva</s> alone' — this is the meaning of 'api' (also). 'There is no absence of that' — this is the meaning of the word 'eva' (indeed). And thus the indicative mark has scope [for application to <s>Viṣṇu</s>], so there is no contradiction — this is the purport. The embodied <s>Ātman</s>, the <s>jīva</s>, at the time of death, 'mounted upon by <s>Prājña</s>' — He who is eminently and thoroughly knowing is <s>Prajña</s> Himself, i.e., <s>Prājña</s> — by that <s>Paramātmā</s>, 'mounted upon' — presided over; as stated in the <s>Bṛhad-bhāṣya</s> (great commentary): 'The Lord goes by mounting upon the <s>jīva</s>'; thus being His vehicle, 'releasing' — abandoning the former body — 'goes' — proceeds to another body and another world — this is the meaning of the <s>Vājasaneya</s> scripture. The meaning of the <s>sūtra</s> is clear.
// Thus ends the <s>Jyotir-adhikaraṇa</s> (Section on Light) // 11 //

